WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.060
You know, when ancient map makers, when they hit the absolute edge of the known world,

00:05.480 --> 00:07.800
they didn't just leave the parchment blank.

00:07.980 --> 00:08.280
Right.

00:08.460 --> 00:09.520
They drew sea monsters.

00:09.840 --> 00:10.220
Exactly.

00:10.400 --> 00:15.160
They would sketch these, like, elaborate, terrifying krakens and leviathans,

00:15.340 --> 00:17.620
and they'd literally write, here be dragons.

00:18.420 --> 00:19.180
It's a great metaphor.

00:19.420 --> 00:24.840
It was basically a deeply human admission of, well, of ignorance.

00:24.960 --> 00:25.640
Yeah, totally.

00:25.800 --> 00:25.920
Yeah.

00:25.920 --> 00:31.160
It's their way of saying, you know, our understanding, our ability to actually observe and document things

00:31.160 --> 00:33.160
has just hit a literal wall.

00:33.520 --> 00:37.520
So everything past that line is just pure, unadulterated speculation.

00:37.940 --> 00:40.900
You reach the limit of your tools, so you substitute mythology.

00:41.180 --> 00:41.580
Exactly.

00:42.000 --> 00:45.580
And, you know, we like to think we've completely outgrown that in modern science.

00:45.680 --> 00:47.040
I mean, we have orbital telescopes.

00:47.260 --> 00:48.660
We have giant particle accelerators.

00:48.880 --> 00:51.560
We've even mapped the cosmic microwave background.

00:51.660 --> 00:52.020
I have.

00:52.020 --> 00:57.260
But if you look at the absolute extremes of astrophysics, you realize very quickly that

00:57.260 --> 00:58.360
we still have our sea monsters.

00:58.700 --> 00:58.800
Yeah.

00:58.800 --> 01:01.540
We just gave them a different, you know, more scientific-sounding name.

01:01.620 --> 01:02.920
We call them singularities.

01:03.260 --> 01:03.500
Right.

01:03.880 --> 01:04.660
We call them black holes.

01:05.540 --> 01:10.520
It's the ultimate conceptual breakdown, a point where the math of general relativity

01:10.520 --> 01:11.820
just implodes.

01:11.980 --> 01:14.060
The density supposedly becomes infinite.

01:14.160 --> 01:14.340
Yeah.

01:14.520 --> 01:20.060
And our greatest theoretical physicists are essentially forced to just draw a dragon on the chalkboard.

01:20.060 --> 01:23.980
The event horizon is basically the modern Here Be Dragons line.

01:24.620 --> 01:27.920
By its very definition, it is a boundary of no return.

01:28.440 --> 01:29.420
You cross it, you're gone.

01:29.760 --> 01:30.260
Exactly.

01:30.740 --> 01:36.140
Once matter or light crosses that threshold, the universe is fundamentally sealed off to us.

01:36.580 --> 01:41.160
I mean, no information can escape to tell us what is actually happening in the interior.

01:41.600 --> 01:43.380
But what if that map is completely wrong?

01:44.020 --> 01:45.400
Like, fundamentally incorrect?

01:45.860 --> 01:48.420
That is the mission for our deep dive today.

01:48.540 --> 01:49.480
It's a massive question.

01:49.480 --> 01:50.160
It really is.

01:50.460 --> 01:56.100
Because for decades, we have taken it as absolute, unquestionable gospel that when a truly massive

01:56.100 --> 02:00.580
star dies and, you know, collapses under the weight of its own gravity, it inevitably

02:00.580 --> 02:03.280
forms a singularity hidden behind an event horizon.

02:03.420 --> 02:04.020
A black hole.

02:04.160 --> 02:04.340
Right.

02:04.640 --> 02:07.520
But today, we are tearing that assumption apart.

02:07.900 --> 02:13.380
We are looking at a deeply provocative, highly technical, independent research paper from

02:13.380 --> 02:14.280
2026.

02:14.560 --> 02:16.240
The author goes by the name Flyxion.

02:16.240 --> 02:19.480
And the title alone really sets the stage.

02:19.620 --> 02:26.100
It's called, uh, Degeneracy Before Collapse, A Falsifiable Horizonless Alternative to Black Hole

02:26.100 --> 02:26.760
Formation.

02:26.920 --> 02:32.040
Which is quite a mouthful, but the central premise of this paper is incredibly bold.

02:32.040 --> 02:33.440
Oh, it's wildly bold.

02:33.440 --> 02:39.220
The author is asking us to consider a reality where the collapsing star never actually forms

02:39.220 --> 02:40.020
an event horizon.

02:40.260 --> 02:41.020
Never forms one at all.

02:41.160 --> 02:41.300
Right.

02:41.720 --> 02:46.460
Instead of collapsing into a mathematical point of infinite density, the matter settles into

02:46.460 --> 02:50.780
a rapidly spinning, incredibly dense, horizonless torus.

02:50.980 --> 02:51.240
A donut.

02:51.520 --> 02:51.820
Basically.

02:51.940 --> 02:52.100
Yeah.

02:52.200 --> 02:52.600
A donut.

02:52.900 --> 02:57.820
An object that, from the outside, looks and behaves almost exactly like a black hole,

02:57.820 --> 03:02.560
but structurally, it completely sidesteps the paradoxes that have kind of broken physics

03:02.560 --> 03:03.700
for the last half century.

03:04.160 --> 03:07.120
A horizonless spinning donut of ultra-dense matter.

03:07.260 --> 03:08.980
I mean, just picturing that is wild.

03:09.180 --> 03:12.580
But if you're listening right now, I really want to emphasize why you should care about

03:12.580 --> 03:12.720
this.

03:12.780 --> 03:14.320
Because it's not just abstract math.

03:14.660 --> 03:15.320
No, exactly.

03:15.440 --> 03:15.560
Yeah.

03:15.560 --> 03:20.820
This deep dive isn't just about some, you know, abstract astrophysics concept that you'll

03:20.820 --> 03:21.700
never interact with.

03:21.840 --> 03:27.160
This paper is a master class in how rigorous, brutal science is actually done.

03:27.340 --> 03:29.180
It's about drawing a razor-sharp line.

03:29.300 --> 03:29.560
Yes.

03:29.560 --> 03:35.360
A line between what we know to be true, what we are just guessing at, and how we build a

03:35.360 --> 03:39.260
theory that has the courage to actually let itself be proven wrong.

03:39.700 --> 03:40.740
Which is huge.

03:40.860 --> 03:40.980
Right.

03:41.040 --> 03:45.140
Because falsifiability is the gold standard of theoretical physics.

03:45.140 --> 03:49.200
And honestly, it is painfully rare in this specific subfield.

03:49.400 --> 03:49.560
Yeah.

03:49.860 --> 03:50.300
Why is that?

03:50.780 --> 03:55.620
Well, when you deal with environments as extreme as black holes, you can easily invent

03:55.620 --> 04:00.100
a mathematical fairy tale that fits your own bias, but it can never actually be tested.

04:00.220 --> 04:01.720
Because it's hidden behind the horizon.

04:01.960 --> 04:02.400
Exactly.

04:02.820 --> 04:08.020
What makes Flyxion's blueprint, this concept of a degenerate Taurus remnant or a DTR, what

04:08.020 --> 04:10.640
makes it so compelling is that it comes with a built-in trap.

04:10.760 --> 04:10.980
Right.

04:10.980 --> 04:14.920
It tells other physicists exactly what to look for to destroy the theory.

04:14.920 --> 04:15.160
Yeah.

04:15.240 --> 04:18.420
So we're going to deconstruct this blueprint piece by piece.

04:19.060 --> 04:24.600
But to understand how a star could possibly halt its own death spiral and avoid becoming

04:24.600 --> 04:28.620
a black hole, we kind of have to start with the fundamental physics of the squeeze.

04:28.960 --> 04:30.300
The limits of compression.

04:30.300 --> 04:30.660
Yeah.

04:30.720 --> 04:35.140
We have to understand the hard limits of how tightly the universe actually allows us to

04:35.140 --> 04:36.200
pack matter together.

04:36.500 --> 04:41.440
So to understand the squeeze, you really have to look at the life of a star as a, well,

04:41.960 --> 04:45.960
a multi-billion year war between two opposing forces.

04:46.240 --> 04:46.900
Gravity and pressure.

04:47.080 --> 04:47.600
Exactly.

04:48.000 --> 04:48.800
Gravity and pressure.

04:49.180 --> 04:52.520
When a star is alive, it's essentially a massive nuclear furnace.

04:52.940 --> 04:57.760
It's constantly crushing hydrogen atoms together to form helium right in its core.

04:57.760 --> 05:02.120
And that nuclear fusion releases an unbelievable amount of outward energy, right?

05:02.220 --> 05:02.400
Right.

05:02.480 --> 05:03.880
Which creates thermal pressure.

05:04.160 --> 05:04.300
Right.

05:04.300 --> 05:08.940
And for millions or even billions of years, that outward thermal pressure perfectly balances

05:08.940 --> 05:12.480
the inward crushing force of the star's immense gravity.

05:12.760 --> 05:13.520
It's a stalemate.

05:13.820 --> 05:14.120
Yes.

05:14.620 --> 05:16.520
We call that hydrostatic equilibrium.

05:17.060 --> 05:18.260
But the fuel isn't infinite.

05:18.260 --> 05:23.820
I mean, eventually the star burns through all its hydrogen, then it moves on to its helium,

05:24.200 --> 05:27.060
moving all the way up the periodic table until it hits iron.

05:27.440 --> 05:30.360
And iron is the death knell for a star.

05:30.700 --> 05:33.420
Because fusing iron doesn't release energy.

05:33.820 --> 05:34.860
It actually absorbs it.

05:35.080 --> 05:35.520
Precisely.

05:36.040 --> 05:37.900
The furnace suddenly shuts off.

05:38.120 --> 05:38.260
Yeah.

05:38.460 --> 05:40.220
That outward pressure just vanishes.

05:40.560 --> 05:45.060
And gravity, which is, you know, relentless and literally never takes a break, instantly takes

05:45.060 --> 05:45.300
over.

05:45.300 --> 05:49.600
The core of the star begins to collapse in on itself in absolute free fall.

05:49.900 --> 05:51.620
The matter gets squeezed incredibly tight.

05:52.140 --> 05:56.900
But nature has a built-in defense mechanism against infinite compression, right?

05:56.980 --> 05:57.460
It does.

05:57.560 --> 06:02.000
It's rooted in quantum mechanics, specifically something called the Pauli Exclusion Principle.

06:02.120 --> 06:02.340
Okay.

06:02.440 --> 06:03.880
Let's break that down for the listener.

06:04.020 --> 06:07.820
Because quantum mechanics can sound like literal magic sometimes.

06:07.920 --> 06:08.120
Sure.

06:08.660 --> 06:14.220
The universe is fundamentally made of two types of particles, bosons and fermions.

06:14.340 --> 06:14.560
Right.

06:14.560 --> 06:20.160
Now, bosons, like photons of light, are essentially the extroverts of the quantum world.

06:20.700 --> 06:25.080
You can stack an infinite number of bosons in the exact same physical space, and they literally

06:25.080 --> 06:25.720
don't care.

06:26.040 --> 06:27.180
But fermions are different.

06:27.540 --> 06:27.700
Yeah.

06:27.860 --> 06:32.840
Fermions, particles like electrons, protons, and neutrons, they are the introverts.

06:32.960 --> 06:33.080
Yeah.

06:33.180 --> 06:39.180
The Pauli Exclusion Principle basically dictates that no two identical fermions can occupy the

06:39.180 --> 06:43.100
exact same quantum state in the same space at the same time.

06:43.100 --> 06:44.300
I really like that framing.

06:44.620 --> 06:45.840
Introverts and extroverts.

06:46.400 --> 06:50.280
Fermions absolutely require their own unique personal space.

06:50.280 --> 06:55.860
So as the star collapses and the density skyrockets, you force these introverted particles closer

06:55.860 --> 06:56.660
and closer together.

06:56.880 --> 06:58.480
And the thermal energy is gone, remember.

06:58.880 --> 07:02.360
But now you have this entirely new kind of resistance.

07:02.800 --> 07:09.640
We call a gas that has been squeezed to this absolute quantum limit a zero temperature fermi gas.

07:09.640 --> 07:10.660
Okay, a fermi gas.

07:10.760 --> 07:10.900
Yeah.

07:11.160 --> 07:16.480
And the resistance they put up simply by refusing to overlap with one another is called degeneracy

07:16.480 --> 07:16.860
pressure.

07:17.100 --> 07:19.900
I always visualize this like a standing room only concert venue.

07:20.040 --> 07:20.660
Oh, that's good.

07:20.740 --> 07:20.860
Right.

07:21.160 --> 07:22.380
So at first the venue is huge.

07:22.700 --> 07:25.220
Everyone has room to move around, dance, maybe throw their arms up.

07:25.280 --> 07:26.400
That's your normal thermal state.

07:26.420 --> 07:27.020
Only a space.

07:27.360 --> 07:31.240
But then imagine the actual walls of the venue start mechanically closing in.

07:31.580 --> 07:32.660
People get pushed together.

07:33.120 --> 07:35.520
Eventually, everyone is packed shoulder to shoulder.

07:35.720 --> 07:36.980
You literally can't move.

07:36.980 --> 07:37.540
Right.

07:37.760 --> 07:41.000
You can't occupy the same physical space as the person next to you.

07:41.520 --> 07:46.200
So they start pushing back against the walls just by the sheer physical fact that they exist

07:46.200 --> 07:47.020
and take up room.

07:47.400 --> 07:49.860
And the math behind that pushback is fascinating.

07:50.540 --> 07:56.700
For a relatively normal, non-relativistic gas, this degeneracy pressure scales with the density

07:56.700 --> 07:58.280
to the power of five-thirds.

07:58.640 --> 07:58.900
Okay.

07:59.100 --> 08:03.860
But as the walls keep closing in, the particles are forced to move faster and faster to find

08:03.860 --> 08:05.440
unoccupied quantum states.

08:05.440 --> 08:07.940
Eventually, they're approaching the speed of light.

08:08.660 --> 08:08.960
Wow.

08:09.220 --> 08:09.380
Yeah.

08:09.660 --> 08:13.900
And in that ultra-relativistic state, the pressure scales differently.

08:14.480 --> 08:16.680
It scales with density to the power of four-thirds.

08:17.200 --> 08:22.400
And depending on the total mass of the dying star, this concert crowd can sometimes actually

08:22.400 --> 08:23.740
win the war against gravity.

08:24.160 --> 08:24.440
Right.

08:24.540 --> 08:29.940
If the collapsing core isn't too massive, the electrons themselves provide enough pushback

08:29.940 --> 08:31.520
to halt the collapse entirely.

08:31.820 --> 08:34.340
The concert crowd holds the walls open.

08:34.340 --> 08:36.440
And the result is a white dwarf.

08:37.040 --> 08:42.120
Which is just a dead star, glowing purely from leftover heat, incredibly dense, and held

08:42.120 --> 08:44.540
up purely by that electron degeneracy pressure.

08:44.760 --> 08:45.040
Yes.

08:45.440 --> 08:48.920
But there's a strict limit to that, the Chandrasekhar mass.

08:49.320 --> 08:49.660
Right.

08:50.100 --> 08:50.860
Tell us about that.

08:51.120 --> 08:54.160
It was calculated by Supermanian Chandrasekhar back in 1930.

08:54.720 --> 08:59.740
He proved mathematically that if the core is heavier than roughly 1.4 times the mass of

08:59.740 --> 09:03.120
our sun, gravity just overpowers the electrons.

09:03.440 --> 09:06.020
The electron degeneracy pressure structurally fails.

09:06.260 --> 09:06.600
Completely.

09:07.080 --> 09:12.280
So the gravity is so intense that the electron concert crowd gets completely crushed.

09:12.640 --> 09:15.940
The electrons get physically forced into the protons, creating neutrons.

09:15.940 --> 09:22.040
And then the venue shrinks again, drastically, until the newly formed neutrons are packed

09:22.040 --> 09:22.960
shoulder to shoulder.

09:23.160 --> 09:27.140
So now you have a star made almost entirely of neutrons, a neutron star.

09:27.260 --> 09:27.580
Exactly.

09:27.800 --> 09:29.960
And this is held up by neutron degeneracy pressure.

09:30.620 --> 09:35.420
Just to give you an idea of the density here, a single teaspoon of neutron star material

09:35.420 --> 09:37.720
would weigh billions of tons on Earth.

09:37.900 --> 09:38.680
That's insane.

09:38.880 --> 09:40.580
But even neutrons have a breaking point, right?

09:40.740 --> 09:41.180
They do.

09:41.620 --> 09:45.760
Physicists call this the Tolman-Oppenheimer-Volkoff limit, or the TOV limit.

09:45.960 --> 09:47.000
The TOV limit.

09:47.640 --> 09:52.060
Just how heavy does a star have to be to literally break the neutrons?

09:52.320 --> 09:56.960
Well, it depends heavily on the specific nuclear equation of state you are using.

09:57.120 --> 09:58.020
Which is what exactly?

09:58.040 --> 10:02.680
It's essentially the mathematical model of how nuclear matter behaves at these extreme

10:02.680 --> 10:03.100
pressures.

10:03.300 --> 10:09.740
But the general consensus puts the TOV limit somewhere between 2 and 2.5 solar masses.

10:10.140 --> 10:10.180
Okay.

10:10.180 --> 10:16.060
If a collapsing core exceeds that limit, there is no known classical equilibrium state to

10:16.060 --> 10:16.540
stop it.

10:17.180 --> 10:19.360
Neutron degeneracy pressure fails.

10:19.640 --> 10:22.060
And standard physics says at that point, nothing stops the walls.

10:22.320 --> 10:23.780
The star continues to collapse.

10:23.900 --> 10:24.960
The event horizon forms.

10:25.040 --> 10:26.300
And you get a singularity.

10:26.380 --> 10:27.040
That block.

10:27.300 --> 10:28.840
That is the standard narrative, yes.

10:29.300 --> 10:33.640
But to understand the alternative, we have to look at the absolute geometric ceiling of

10:33.640 --> 10:35.020
how dense an object can be.

10:35.080 --> 10:36.880
This is theorem 1 in our source material, right?

10:36.880 --> 10:37.260
Yes.

10:37.800 --> 10:38.840
Established by H.A.

10:38.900 --> 10:40.320
Buchdahl in 1959.

10:41.040 --> 10:45.620
Buchdahl wasn't concerned with whether the star was made of electrons or neutrons or something

10:45.620 --> 10:46.720
we haven't even discovered yet.

10:46.760 --> 10:47.840
You didn't care about the material.

10:48.060 --> 10:48.380
Exactly.

10:48.540 --> 10:51.460
He was looking at the fundamental geometry of space-time itself.

10:51.820 --> 10:56.300
So Buchdahl essentially said, I don't care what magical material your star is made of.

10:56.300 --> 10:58.740
I don't care about your equation of state.

10:59.280 --> 11:03.880
There is a fundamental geometric limit to how compact a static object can be.

11:04.040 --> 11:04.320
Yes.

11:04.900 --> 11:07.720
And to grasp this, we really need to define compactness.

11:08.080 --> 11:09.880
The paper uses the parameter c.

11:10.040 --> 11:10.240
Okay.

11:10.400 --> 11:10.980
Parameter c.

11:11.080 --> 11:11.720
Let's define it.

11:11.860 --> 11:14.320
The parameter c is the dimensionless ratio.

11:14.820 --> 11:17.920
It is defined as 2gm over rc squared.

11:18.100 --> 11:19.620
Let's unpack those variables for everyone.

11:19.680 --> 11:19.860
Sure.

11:20.580 --> 11:22.500
G is Newton's gravitational constant.

11:23.060 --> 11:24.780
M is the total mass of the star.

11:25.180 --> 11:26.940
R is the physical radius of the star.

11:27.060 --> 11:28.300
And c is the speed of light.

11:28.480 --> 11:28.620
Okay.

11:28.880 --> 11:33.040
As a star collapses, its mass stays roughly the same, but its radius shrinks.

11:33.480 --> 11:37.900
So as the denominator, r, gets smaller, the compactness c increases.

11:38.140 --> 11:43.400
Well, a normal star, like our sun, has a very low compactness because its radius is huge.

11:43.540 --> 11:43.980
Exactly.

11:43.980 --> 11:45.820
A neutron star has a much higher compactness.

11:45.900 --> 11:46.220
Exactly.

11:46.220 --> 11:50.440
And a black hole, where the radius has shrunk all the way down to the Schwarzschild radius,

11:50.580 --> 11:55.860
which is the exact location of the event horizon, that compactness parameter c is exactly 1.

11:56.240 --> 11:57.180
Exactly 1.

11:57.780 --> 12:00.840
A black hole is the definition of maximum compactness.

12:01.560 --> 12:08.280
What Buchdahl proved is that for any static, spherically symmetric star, under standard conditions,

12:08.860 --> 12:15.000
the absolute maximum compactness is c is less than or equal to 8 ninths.

12:15.000 --> 12:16.240
8 ninths.

12:16.560 --> 12:17.860
You cannot reach 1.

12:17.900 --> 12:19.220
8 ninths, not 1.

12:19.400 --> 12:21.580
Let's make this tangible with an analogy because I think it helps.

12:21.640 --> 12:21.940
Please do.

12:22.320 --> 12:26.700
Imagine you're trying to pack a perfectly spherical suitcase with clothes,

12:26.960 --> 12:32.580
and you're pushing down on the shell of the suitcase from all sides equally to zip it shut.

12:32.680 --> 12:33.740
Okay, I can picture that.

12:33.820 --> 12:39.280
Inside, the clothes are squished, and they are pushing outward against the inside of the suitcase equally in all directions.

12:39.280 --> 12:42.200
That is what physicists call isotropic pressure.

12:42.300 --> 12:43.720
Equal pressure in every direction.

12:43.920 --> 12:44.020
Right.

12:44.380 --> 12:48.580
Buchdahl's math proves that if the pressure inside that suitcase is perfectly equal everywhere,

12:49.000 --> 12:52.680
you will hit a strict geometric limit 8 ninths of maximum density.

12:52.800 --> 12:54.080
You can't pack it any tighter.

12:54.500 --> 12:54.880
Exactly.

12:54.880 --> 12:58.640
If you try to force the zipper closed past that 8 ninths mark,

12:59.120 --> 13:04.760
the internal pressure required to keep the suitcase stable literally breaks the laws of physics.

13:05.480 --> 13:11.380
The universe will not allow you to zip that suitcase any tighter while remaining a static, physical object.

13:11.540 --> 13:11.820
Right.

13:11.820 --> 13:17.920
The math dictates that to push past an 8 ninths compactness with an isotropic fluid,

13:18.340 --> 13:22.640
the pressure at the absolute center of the star would have to become infinite.

13:22.900 --> 13:23.600
Which is impossible.

13:23.740 --> 13:26.500
Or, alternatively, the pressure would have to become negative,

13:26.900 --> 13:30.260
meaning the fluid would have to somehow pull inward instead of pushing outward.

13:30.420 --> 13:32.540
Which also makes no sense for normal matter.

13:32.880 --> 13:37.260
Both of these scenarios violate basic energy conditions and classical physics.

13:37.260 --> 13:43.460
Therefore, reaching a compactness of 1 with an isotropic fluid is provably impossible.

13:43.580 --> 13:43.860
Wait, wait.

13:44.300 --> 13:48.960
If standard physics says you literally cannot reach a compactness of 1 without breaking the universe,

13:49.420 --> 13:51.680
how do we ever get black holes?

13:51.980 --> 13:52.320
Ah.

13:52.820 --> 13:54.200
And more to the point of this deep dive,

13:54.520 --> 13:57.720
how does Flyxion propose we get an object that mimics a black hole

13:57.720 --> 14:00.100
without triggering these exact mathematical violations?

14:00.540 --> 14:04.260
The answer is buried in the specific assumptions of Buchdahl's proof.

14:04.260 --> 14:08.460
His theorem relies on the premise that the pressure is isotropic.

14:08.700 --> 14:09.580
Equal in all directions.

14:09.680 --> 14:10.840
Equal in all directions.

14:11.080 --> 14:15.580
But what if the clothes inside the suitcase aren't pushing outward against the zipper equally?

14:15.900 --> 14:19.380
What if they're pushing sideways against each other harder than they are pushing outward?

14:19.620 --> 14:23.080
You've just independently discovered the Bowers-Liang loophole.

14:23.640 --> 14:25.540
This is theorem 2 in the paper.

14:26.000 --> 14:31.100
It references a landmark 1974 study by R.L. Bowers and E.P.T. Liang.

14:31.100 --> 14:37.040
They mathematically demonstrated that if you just drop the assumption of isotropy...

14:37.040 --> 14:38.420
So if the pressures are unequal.

14:38.580 --> 14:38.820
Right.

14:39.080 --> 14:42.220
If the radial pressure, which is pushing straight out from the center,

14:42.320 --> 14:45.500
is not equal to the tangential pressure, which is pushing sideways,

14:46.240 --> 14:49.500
the Buchdahl bound completely vanishes.

14:49.720 --> 14:50.440
Just disappears.

14:50.560 --> 14:52.420
It completely ceases to be a barrier.

14:52.420 --> 14:58.480
By allowing for anisotropic pressure, you can theoretically construct a static configuration of matter

14:58.480 --> 15:01.320
with a compactness arbitrarily close to 1.

15:01.660 --> 15:05.840
And the brilliant part is that you can do it using ordinary matter with positive energy.

15:06.060 --> 15:10.080
You do not have to violate the null or weak energy conditions.

15:10.180 --> 15:12.120
As long as the anisotropy scales correctly, right?

15:12.400 --> 15:12.620
Exactly.

15:13.160 --> 15:18.020
Provided that the anisotropy, that difference between the sideways pressure and the outward pressure,

15:18.500 --> 15:20.960
scales appropriately toward the center of the object.

15:20.960 --> 15:25.420
So just by allowing the internal pressure of the star to be unequal,

15:25.780 --> 15:29.060
we can pack that cosmic suitcase almost all the way to 1.

15:29.200 --> 15:29.560
Yes.

15:29.880 --> 15:33.940
We can create an object that is incredibly dense, incredibly compact,

15:34.280 --> 15:38.860
but still fundamentally a physical object made of matter, not a singularity.

15:39.000 --> 15:39.840
That's the theory.

15:40.120 --> 15:43.900
But if we want to claim that this object prevents a black hole from forming,

15:44.300 --> 15:46.360
we have to deal with the giant of the field.

15:46.360 --> 15:51.240
We have to face the 1965 Singularity Theorem by Roger Penrose.

15:51.380 --> 15:52.120
Oh, absolutely.

15:52.520 --> 15:55.140
Roger Penrose was awarded a Nobel Prize for this work.

15:55.560 --> 15:59.020
It is the absolute bedrock of modern gravitational physics.

15:59.240 --> 16:00.640
Theorem 3 in our source document.

16:00.760 --> 16:00.860
Right.

16:01.280 --> 16:03.340
Theorem 3 outlines Penrose's conclusion.

16:03.960 --> 16:08.360
Under a specific set of conditions, gravitational collapse must lead to a singularity.

16:08.380 --> 16:08.920
It's guaranteed.

16:08.920 --> 16:09.560
Yes.

16:10.220 --> 16:14.600
In the formal language of relativity, he proved that the space-time becomes, quote,

16:15.240 --> 16:17.480
future null geodesically incomplete.

16:18.600 --> 16:21.060
Future null geodesically incomplete.

16:21.460 --> 16:21.940
I know.

16:22.100 --> 16:25.960
That sounds like a phrase a politician would use to avoid answering a direct question.

16:26.860 --> 16:28.400
Let's strip the jargon out.

16:28.660 --> 16:29.680
What does that actually mean?

16:29.680 --> 16:35.540
Okay, so a geodesic is the straightest possible path a particle can take through curved space-time.

16:36.220 --> 16:42.080
A null geodesic is specifically the path that a massless particle, like a photon of light, takes.

16:42.240 --> 16:42.700
A light grade.

16:42.760 --> 16:42.920
Right.

16:43.260 --> 16:49.740
To say a space-time is null geodesically incomplete means that a ray of light traveling forward in time just ends.

16:49.800 --> 16:50.580
It just hits a wall.

16:50.680 --> 16:57.300
It hits a boundary or a point of infinite space-time curvature where the path physically cannot be extended any further.

16:57.300 --> 17:02.920
That abrupt mathematical termination of the light's path is the signature of a singularity.

17:03.240 --> 17:04.240
The map breaks.

17:04.480 --> 17:07.040
But Penrose didn't just casually declare that this always happens.

17:07.380 --> 17:12.580
His genius was proving that it happens conditionally based on three very specific prerequisites.

17:12.700 --> 17:12.920
Yes.

17:13.020 --> 17:17.700
If you have A, B, and C, you are mathematically guaranteed to get a singularity.

17:17.760 --> 17:18.100
Correct.

17:18.480 --> 17:20.900
Three separable strict hypotheses.

17:20.900 --> 17:29.880
First, the space-time must be globally hyperbolic with a non-compact Cauchy surface.

17:29.980 --> 17:33.160
Okay, let's unpack that because Cauchy surfaces are conceptually fascinating.

17:33.760 --> 17:38.260
I picture a Cauchy surface like a single frame in a movie reel.

17:38.480 --> 17:39.660
That's a good way to look at it.

17:39.720 --> 17:45.100
It is a snapshot of the entire universe at one exact instantaneous moment in time.

17:45.240 --> 17:46.360
That's a perfect visualization.

17:46.360 --> 17:51.360
If a space-time is globally hyperbolic, it means the universe is deeply deterministic.

17:52.120 --> 17:52.480
Predictable.

17:52.720 --> 17:53.160
Exactly.

17:53.580 --> 18:00.180
It means if I hand you that single frame of the movie, if you know the position and momentum of every particle on that Cauchy surface,

18:00.820 --> 18:07.740
the laws of physics allow you to perfectly calculate the next frame and the next, all the way into the future and all the way into the past.

18:07.800 --> 18:08.980
It means the universe makes sense.

18:08.980 --> 18:15.260
There are no closed, time-like curves, no time travel back to the past to become your own grandfather and break the timeline.

18:15.560 --> 18:17.760
The math just flows predictably forward.

18:18.020 --> 18:18.340
Exactly.

18:18.640 --> 18:20.140
Predictable space-time is condition one.

18:20.320 --> 18:22.540
Condition two is the null energy condition.

18:22.960 --> 18:27.640
This is a mathematical formalization of the idea that gravity is universally attractive.

18:28.520 --> 18:35.600
It states that for any null vector, the local energy density measured by an observer moving at the speed of light cannot be negative.

18:35.600 --> 18:39.080
Matter has positive energy, and therefore it pulls things together.

18:39.180 --> 18:40.140
It doesn't push them apart.

18:40.400 --> 18:41.360
Gravity tracks.

18:42.180 --> 18:44.420
So condition two is attractive gravity.

18:45.000 --> 18:46.700
What's the final piece of the puzzle?

18:46.980 --> 18:50.940
Condition three is the existence of a closed, trapped surface.

18:51.860 --> 18:53.260
This is the point of no return.

18:53.500 --> 18:54.560
The event horizon, basically.

18:54.900 --> 18:56.040
Well, closely related.

18:56.040 --> 19:05.200
It is a two-dimensional surface in space where gravity has become so overwhelmingly strong that outward-pointing light rays are actually dragged inward.

19:05.600 --> 19:06.100
Okay, wait.

19:06.200 --> 19:06.860
Walk me through that.

19:07.180 --> 19:09.280
Imagine a sphere emitting a flash of light.

19:09.740 --> 19:12.400
Normally, that sphere of light expands outward, growing larger.

19:12.700 --> 19:20.060
But on a closed, trapped surface, the gravity is so severe that the surface area of that outward flash of light is actually decreasing.

19:20.360 --> 19:20.700
Wow.

19:20.900 --> 19:22.540
So the light is falling back in on itself.

19:22.540 --> 19:28.720
And if you have all three, a predictable universe, attractive gravity, and a closed, trapped surface,

19:29.820 --> 19:32.520
Penrose proved that a singularity is unavoidable.

19:33.020 --> 19:35.280
The light paths are literally forced to end.

19:35.280 --> 19:35.640
Yes.

19:36.300 --> 19:39.840
Now, Appendix A of Flyxion's paper does something really important here.

19:40.300 --> 19:43.520
It doesn't just ask us to take Penrose's Nobel-winning word for it.

19:44.000 --> 19:50.400
The author uses something called the Raichoturi equation to show us the actual mechanics of how the light rays are forced to end.

19:50.860 --> 19:53.980
Amal Kumar Raichoturi published this equation in 1955.

19:54.780 --> 19:58.760
It is essentially the geometric engine running under the hood of Penrose's theorem.

19:59.020 --> 19:59.540
How so?

19:59.540 --> 20:08.340
It describes how a bundle of light rays, a congruence of null geodesics, mathematically speaking, evolves as it travels through curved spacetime.

20:08.340 --> 20:11.720
I want to visualize this without reading a bunch of algebraic fractions.

20:12.320 --> 20:15.480
Imagine you're in a pitch black room and you turn on a standard flashlight.

20:15.720 --> 20:15.920
Okay.

20:16.200 --> 20:20.420
The beam of light leaves the bulb and spreads out, getting wider as it hits the far wall.

20:20.780 --> 20:26.220
The Raichoturi equation is essentially tracking the geometry of that beam, right?

20:26.220 --> 20:32.200
Yes, and the equation breaks the deformation of that beam down into three geometric components.

20:33.060 --> 20:35.980
First is expansion, denoted as theta.

20:36.060 --> 20:36.340
Theta.

20:36.700 --> 20:39.420
If the beam is getting wider, expansion is positive.

20:39.760 --> 20:42.040
If the beam is narrowing, expansion is negative.

20:42.380 --> 20:43.020
Simple enough.

20:43.280 --> 20:44.180
Second is shear.

20:44.360 --> 20:51.200
That's if the circular beam of the flashlight gets distorted into an oval or an ellipse without the total area actually changing.

20:51.320 --> 20:51.680
Exactly.

20:51.680 --> 20:57.020
And third is twist, which is exactly what it sounds like, the bundle of light rays spiraling around each other.

20:57.560 --> 21:04.500
Now, the paper simplifies the scenario by specifying that the congruence of light rays is hypersurface orthogonal.

21:04.980 --> 21:05.280
What do you mean?

21:05.440 --> 21:09.580
In physics geometry, this mathematically forces the twist to be exactly zero.

21:10.200 --> 21:11.480
The rays aren't spiraling.

21:11.820 --> 21:12.000
Yeah.

21:12.080 --> 21:18.600
Furthermore, the shear squared is a sum of squares, meaning it is mathematically forced to always be positive or zero.

21:18.600 --> 21:21.600
Which isolates the most important part of the Raichoturi equation.

21:22.200 --> 21:24.200
The rate of change of the expansion over time.

21:24.360 --> 21:24.600
Right.

21:25.200 --> 21:32.780
If you assume the null energy condition holds, meaning gravity is attractive, the Raichoturi equation boils down to a strict inequality.

21:32.960 --> 21:33.140
Okay.

21:33.240 --> 21:33.880
What does it say?

21:34.180 --> 21:38.220
It dictates that the rate of change of the expansion is always negative and accelerating.

21:39.060 --> 21:44.720
If the beam is narrowing, attractive gravity guarantees it will narrow faster and faster.

21:44.880 --> 21:47.620
So the gravity acts like a cosmic magnifying glass.

21:47.620 --> 21:56.040
It takes that narrowing beam of light and violently focuses it down to a single, infinitely sharp point.

21:56.340 --> 21:58.640
We call that focal point a conjugate point.

21:59.280 --> 22:06.620
The Raichoturi equation, because of its differential structure, guarantees that the expansion goes to negative infinity within a finite distance.

22:06.620 --> 22:10.380
The light rays physically cross each other at that conjugate point.

22:10.680 --> 22:11.120
Exactly.

22:11.520 --> 22:13.760
But crossing light rays isn't a singularity.

22:13.960 --> 22:17.260
I mean, I can cross two laser pointers in my living room and I don't break the universe.

22:17.440 --> 22:17.580
Yeah.

22:17.760 --> 22:19.640
Where does the singularity actually come in?

22:19.640 --> 22:23.240
That is where Penrose's global topological arguments come into play.

22:23.840 --> 22:28.360
A closed, trapped surface, by definition, means the light rays are already narrowing.

22:28.740 --> 22:30.280
The expansion is already negative.

22:30.700 --> 22:34.300
The Raichoturi equation guarantees those rays will hit a conjugate point.

22:35.000 --> 22:40.480
Penrose showed that once the light rays forming the boundary of that trapped region cross at the conjugate point,

22:40.860 --> 22:44.080
they can no longer remain on the boundary of the region's causal future.

22:44.440 --> 22:45.180
Wait, so...

22:45.180 --> 22:49.300
If you try to mathematically extend the path of the light rays past that crossing point,

22:49.520 --> 22:52.540
you introduce contradictions that violate the first condition.

22:52.700 --> 22:54.780
The global predictability of the space-time.

22:54.860 --> 22:55.400
Exactly.

22:55.940 --> 22:57.940
The Kachi surface flipbook stops working.

22:58.360 --> 22:59.800
You cannot calculate the next stream.

23:00.460 --> 23:01.820
The geodesic is incomplete.

23:02.220 --> 23:02.980
The map breaks.

23:03.080 --> 23:04.620
You have to draw a sea monster.

23:04.800 --> 23:07.700
It is a stunning, airtight piece of deductive logic.

23:08.320 --> 23:10.960
A plus B plus C equals singularity.

23:10.980 --> 23:11.880
It really is.

23:11.880 --> 23:16.140
But the beauty of formal logic is that it shows you the exact escape routes.

23:17.100 --> 23:20.720
If the theorem relies on three distinct pillars to support the singularity,

23:21.260 --> 23:24.040
then preventing the singularity is conceptually simple.

23:24.260 --> 23:24.480
Yeah.

23:24.720 --> 23:26.100
You just have to kick down one of the pillars.

23:26.240 --> 23:26.440
Right.

23:26.720 --> 23:30.560
If null completeness implies not A or not B or not C,

23:30.760 --> 23:35.560
then the paper outlines two main physical routes theorists take to break the theorem.

23:35.860 --> 23:39.440
Let's start with root A, what the author calls the interior route.

23:39.440 --> 23:41.840
Root A acknowledges the collapse.

23:42.480 --> 23:44.060
It says, let the gravity win.

23:44.600 --> 23:46.460
Let the closed trap surface form.

23:46.640 --> 23:49.300
Let the event horizon enclose the dying star.

23:49.460 --> 23:49.640
Okay.

23:49.860 --> 23:50.880
Let it become a black hole.

23:51.080 --> 23:53.640
But once you are safely hidden inside the horizon,

23:54.200 --> 23:56.220
you break the null energy condition.

23:56.320 --> 23:57.380
You break condition two.

23:57.740 --> 23:59.600
You say gravity stops being attractive.

23:59.760 --> 24:00.280
Exactly.

24:00.640 --> 24:03.120
Theeons like gravistars take this exact approach.

24:03.120 --> 24:08.280
They postulate that the interior of the black hole is filled with an exotic dark energy

24:08.280 --> 24:12.480
or a classical stress tensor that has extreme negative pressure.

24:12.880 --> 24:13.420
Negative pressure.

24:13.580 --> 24:13.900
Yes.

24:14.340 --> 24:18.160
This negative energy exerts a repulsive gravitational force at the core,

24:18.600 --> 24:22.360
halting the final collapse into a singularity just inside the horizon.

24:22.720 --> 24:28.000
See, my problem with root A is that it feels fundamentally unscientific from an observation perspective.

24:28.100 --> 24:28.640
Oh, so...

24:28.640 --> 24:32.900
If the event horizon forms, the object is sealed off from the rest of the universe.

24:33.500 --> 24:34.820
It's behind an invisible curtain.

24:35.760 --> 24:40.900
The theorists can put whatever magical negative energy fairy dust they want inside the gravistar,

24:41.460 --> 24:47.320
but no causal signal can ever escape the horizon to reach our telescopes and prove them right.

24:47.580 --> 24:49.280
That's a very fair critique.

24:49.560 --> 24:53.580
It's mathematically interesting, but it's functionally unobservable.

24:53.580 --> 24:57.660
The paper also touches on fuzzballs, which emerge from string theory.

24:57.660 --> 25:00.980
Fuzzballs take a slightly different angle on root A.

25:01.200 --> 25:02.240
Do they use negative energy?

25:02.460 --> 25:04.620
They don't rely on negative classical energy.

25:05.080 --> 25:09.740
Instead, they kick down condition 1, the predictable classical geometry.

25:09.980 --> 25:11.660
Ah, the Kachi surface.

25:11.760 --> 25:12.020
Right.

25:12.400 --> 25:15.160
They argue that as you approach the would-be singularity,

25:15.440 --> 25:21.680
classical spacetime breaks down entirely and is replaced by a chaotic quantum microstate of vibrating strings

25:21.680 --> 25:24.180
that extends all the way out to the horizon scale.

25:24.180 --> 25:31.240
But the debate still stands whether a fuzzball effectively acts as a causal barrier similar to an event horizon.

25:31.540 --> 25:32.080
Yes, it does.

25:32.380 --> 25:35.160
Which brings us to root B, the horizonless route.

25:36.020 --> 25:38.540
This is the path Flyxion aggressively advocates for.

25:39.020 --> 25:41.420
How does root B dismantle Penrose?

25:41.960 --> 25:44.780
Root B says we aren't going to invent negative energy.

25:44.940 --> 25:46.780
We are going to keep gravity attractive.

25:47.020 --> 25:49.940
We are going to maintain classical spacetime geometry.

25:50.300 --> 25:52.840
So we leave pillars 1 and 2 completely intact.

25:52.840 --> 25:56.360
Exactly. The only logical option left is to kick down Pillar 3.

25:57.160 --> 26:00.820
We prevent the closed, trapped surface from ever forming in the first place.

26:00.900 --> 26:03.000
We halt the collapse before the point of no return.

26:03.100 --> 26:08.200
Exactly. The remnant of the star never actually compresses past its own Schwarzschild radius.

26:08.680 --> 26:14.380
It settles into a static or stationary state of equilibrium where the compactness is strictly less than 1.

26:14.620 --> 26:15.920
But it could be close, right?

26:15.920 --> 26:19.900
It might be incredibly close, 1.99999999, but it is not one.

26:20.320 --> 26:26.820
No trapped surface forms, which means no event horizon forms, which means Penrose's initial condition is never met.

26:27.280 --> 26:29.520
The singularity theorem simply doesn't apply.

26:29.740 --> 26:35.320
And we know exactly how they propose to halt that collapse because we established the physics in the suitcase analogy.

26:35.320 --> 26:36.600
Anisotropic pressure.

26:36.600 --> 26:38.440
The Bowers-Laying loophole.

26:38.840 --> 26:53.380
If the inward crushing gravity is met with an unequal pressure that pushes sideways harder than it pushes out, the Bouffdal bound is bypassed and the star can achieve that extreme black hole-like compactness without ever crossing the threshold.

26:53.380 --> 26:56.380
That is the mechanical foundation of Flyxion's proposal.

26:56.900 --> 27:02.040
Because there is no event horizon, this root B object has a physical, material surface.

27:02.660 --> 27:05.640
It remains in causal contact with the rest of the universe.

27:05.900 --> 27:07.440
We can actually point a telescope at it.

27:07.560 --> 27:08.460
We can interact with it.

27:08.920 --> 27:16.840
Okay, so we've established that this root B object is physically permissible within the bounds of general relativity, provided we use anisotropic pressure.

27:17.100 --> 27:17.380
Right.

27:17.560 --> 27:19.240
But what does this thing actually look like?

27:19.280 --> 27:20.920
What is a degenerate Taurus remnant?

27:20.920 --> 27:27.460
The formal definition provided by the author is a stationary, axisymmetric, rotating configuration of degenerate matter.

27:27.800 --> 27:29.660
Let's translate the geometry for everyone.

27:29.820 --> 27:32.660
Stationary means the overall structure isn't changing over time.

27:33.200 --> 27:36.520
Axisymmetric means it has rotational symmetry around a central axis.

27:37.140 --> 27:39.700
In short, it's spinning incredibly fast.

27:40.000 --> 27:44.380
And to help us visualize it, the paper uses a very specific morphological analogy.

27:44.880 --> 27:47.180
It compares the DTR to a synestria.

27:47.280 --> 27:47.920
A synestria.

27:47.920 --> 27:50.200
Now, I'm familiar with synestrias from planetary science.

27:50.580 --> 28:01.480
A synestria is what forms during a massive cataclysmic planetary collision, like the impact between the proto-Earth and a Mars-sized body that scientists believe formed our moon.

28:01.540 --> 28:02.880
Right, a massive impact.

28:02.980 --> 28:06.700
The kinetic energy of the crash is so extreme that it vaporizes the rock.

28:06.700 --> 28:15.980
And because the system has so much angular momentum, so much spin, that cloud of vaporized rock flattens out into a massive, glowing, red-hot torus.

28:16.520 --> 28:16.960
A donut.

28:17.220 --> 28:18.580
Yes, that is a synestria.

28:18.820 --> 28:21.380
But the paper issues a very explicit warning here.

28:21.740 --> 28:25.240
The comparison to a synestria is strictly a morphological analogy.

28:25.500 --> 28:26.140
Just the shape.

28:26.140 --> 28:28.240
It is a comparison of shape, nothing else.

28:28.840 --> 28:38.060
Both the synestria and the DTR are rotating bodies where the sheer speed of rotation relative to their self-gravity forces the equilibrium shape to become toroidal.

28:38.320 --> 28:41.820
But the underlying physics are separated by orders of magnitude.

28:42.180 --> 28:47.740
Right, because a planetary synestria is basically a superheated cloud of rock vapor held up by thermal pressure.

28:48.460 --> 28:50.000
It's fluffy on a clasmic scale.

28:50.320 --> 28:51.020
Very fluffy.

28:51.020 --> 28:59.060
But the DTR is made of ultra-compressed degenerate matter held up by quantum degeneracy pressure and severe pressure anisotropy.

28:59.640 --> 29:02.180
It is millions of times denser.

29:02.300 --> 29:07.720
It's like comparing a cloud of steam to a solid ring of titanium just because they both happen to be circular.

29:07.920 --> 29:08.400
Exactly.

29:09.000 --> 29:12.100
The mechanics of the shape are driven by centrifugal force.

29:12.100 --> 29:21.680
As the original stellar core collapses and spins faster and faster, conserving its angular momentum like an ice skater pulling their arms in it,

29:22.120 --> 29:28.000
the centrifugal force eventually pushes the matter outward away from the central axis of rotation.

29:28.400 --> 29:29.300
So what happens to the center?

29:29.460 --> 29:35.860
The center of the object essentially hollows out and the mass gets concentrated into an impossibly dense equatorial ring.

29:36.000 --> 29:39.100
I picture it like an Italian chef spinning pizza dough in the air.

29:39.260 --> 29:40.140
That works perfectly.

29:40.140 --> 29:44.360
When you toss and spin the dough, the centrifugal force pushes the mass outward.

29:44.880 --> 29:50.700
The center of the dough gets incredibly thin, sometimes tearing open entirely, while the outside edge forms a thick, dense crust.

29:50.980 --> 29:56.340
The DTR is like spinning the ultimate cosmic pizza dough until the center is a total vacuum.

29:56.760 --> 30:00.380
And an entire star's work of mass is packed into the crust.

30:01.080 --> 30:02.800
That's a highly accurate visualization.

30:03.160 --> 30:03.320
Yeah.

30:03.440 --> 30:08.380
The question then becomes, exactly where does this dense crust sit in space?

30:08.440 --> 30:09.360
Okay, where does it sit?

30:09.360 --> 30:15.760
The paper defines the physical material boundary of the DTR using an aerial radius denoted as R sub-zero.

30:16.320 --> 30:18.540
And the mathematical definition is crucial here.

30:18.960 --> 30:23.140
R sub-zero equals R plus times the quantity one plus epsilon.

30:23.480 --> 30:24.280
Okay, math time.

30:24.400 --> 30:25.720
Let's break that formula down.

30:25.940 --> 30:26.940
What is R plus?

30:27.040 --> 30:32.840
R plus represents the radius of the outer event horizon if this object were a standard Kerr black hole.

30:33.240 --> 30:34.360
Kerr meaning rotating.

30:34.360 --> 30:34.840
Exactly.

30:35.020 --> 30:37.760
A Kerr black hole is the metric for rotating singularity.

30:38.120 --> 30:43.860
So R plus is the exact location where the point of no return would sit if the star had fully collapsed.

30:44.000 --> 30:44.980
It's the cliff edge.

30:45.280 --> 30:45.460
Yes.

30:45.460 --> 30:46.260
And what is epsilon?

30:46.660 --> 30:50.460
Epsilon is a parameter representing a very small positive number.

30:50.860 --> 30:54.920
So 1 plus epsilon means that the physical boundary of the DTR, the outer edge of the donut,

30:55.420 --> 30:59.320
sits just a microscopic fraction of the distance outside where the event horizon would have formed.

30:59.320 --> 31:03.500
It stops its collapse right at the absolute brink of the cliff.

31:03.980 --> 31:04.380
Right there.

31:04.720 --> 31:05.220
Now hold up.

31:05.360 --> 31:06.900
This is where I have to push back a little bit.

31:07.000 --> 31:07.540
Go for it.

31:07.660 --> 31:13.540
I am trying to view this from the perspective of our listeners, and quite frankly, this sounds incredibly convenient.

31:14.180 --> 31:19.880
They are just parking this donut a millimeter away from the event horizon to make the math work.

31:20.020 --> 31:20.420
Well.

31:20.420 --> 31:29.540
The paper says if we want this DTR to perfectly mimic a black hole from the outside so that our telescopes can't tell the difference,

31:30.280 --> 31:33.660
epsilon has to be vanishingly small, approaching zero.

31:33.740 --> 31:34.000
Yes.

31:34.580 --> 31:35.060
It does.

31:35.240 --> 31:41.100
But if epsilon gets that small, the pressure and isotropy required to keep the donut from collapsing becomes staggering.

31:41.380 --> 31:41.660
Mm-hmm.

31:42.240 --> 31:44.700
Are they just inventing magic matter to make this work?

31:44.700 --> 31:50.640
That is the exact right question to ask, and it is a vulnerability the author addresses openly in section 4.2.

31:50.700 --> 31:51.000
They do.

31:51.260 --> 31:51.600
They do.

31:52.120 --> 31:58.380
Flyxion concedes that while the Bowers-Liang mechanism works perfectly with normal, well-understood physics at larger values of epsilon,

31:58.960 --> 32:01.880
pushing epsilon to the extreme limit requires an assumption.

32:02.140 --> 32:02.680
What assumption?

32:03.180 --> 32:09.520
They explicitly postulate that sustaining this required pressure and isotropy at a vanishingly small epsilon

32:09.520 --> 32:13.620
might require an unknown supernuclear equation of state.

32:13.620 --> 32:17.960
Wait, a supernuclear equation of state, isn't that just a fancy way of saying,

32:18.400 --> 32:21.600
we don't know if matter can actually do this, so we're going to guess that it can?

32:21.720 --> 32:22.140
I mean...

32:22.140 --> 32:27.860
How is that any better than Ru-A inventing negative energy inside the Gravistar?

32:28.020 --> 32:32.900
It feels like the same sin just committed on the outside of the horizon instead of the inside.

32:33.300 --> 32:37.280
I understand the skepticism, but there is a fundamental methodological difference here.

32:37.380 --> 32:37.740
Which is?

32:37.740 --> 32:43.100
Ru-A requires a violation of the null energy condition, a foundational pillar of relativity.

32:43.620 --> 32:46.840
Flyxion is not proposing a violation of energy conditions.

32:46.880 --> 32:47.560
Okay, that's true.

32:47.760 --> 32:52.700
They are pointing out that our empirical knowledge of how dense matter behaves our nuclear equations of state

32:52.700 --> 32:55.700
literally stops at the core density of a neutron star.

32:56.360 --> 33:02.380
We simply do not have particle accelerators powerful enough or astrophysical measurements precise enough

33:02.380 --> 33:09.100
to know exactly how matter behaves at densities far beyond the TOV limit but just shy of black hole compactness.

33:09.100 --> 33:14.280
So they are saying the physics might exist, we just haven't unlocked the tech tree to see it yet.

33:14.480 --> 33:15.040
Precisely.

33:15.400 --> 33:23.360
They are relying on a known, mathematically proven loophole anisotropy and extrapolating it into an unknown regime.

33:23.800 --> 33:26.420
But the author is intellectually honest about it.

33:26.480 --> 33:26.700
Right.

33:26.700 --> 33:28.040
They frame it as a postulate.

33:28.040 --> 33:35.800
They state clearly that if future research proves that ordinary matter absolutely cannot sustain that level of anisotropy,

33:36.080 --> 33:43.840
then the DTR theory might be forced to adopt the exotic physics of root A, at which point it loses its distinct advantage.

33:44.320 --> 33:49.440
It's a calculated theoretical gamble, but one constrained by observable physics.

33:49.440 --> 33:51.620
Okay. I'll accept that as a fair hypothesis.

33:51.820 --> 33:52.100
Okay.

33:52.260 --> 33:59.240
Because if that gamble pays off, if this super nuclear equation of state actually exists and the DTR can balance perfectly on the edge of the cliff,

33:59.580 --> 34:00.800
the implications are staggering.

34:00.960 --> 34:01.340
They are.

34:01.640 --> 34:07.600
If this horizonless donut is real, it solves one of the most vicious longstanding headaches in modern physics.

34:07.980 --> 34:10.720
Let's talk about the information paradox and deep time evaporation.

34:10.720 --> 34:17.380
The black hole information paradox is a crisis that fundamentally pits the two most successful theories in human history,

34:18.040 --> 34:22.560
quantum mechanics and general relativity, against each other in a fight to the death.

34:22.800 --> 34:25.100
Wow. Okay. Set the stage for us.

34:25.540 --> 34:29.680
In quantum mechanics, a non-negotiable law is that information is conserved.

34:30.240 --> 34:33.200
The universe is deterministic at a quantum level.

34:33.520 --> 34:39.580
To give a classic example, if I take a handwritten journal and throw it into a campfire, it looks like the information is gone.

34:39.580 --> 34:41.160
Right. The pages turn to ash.

34:41.440 --> 34:48.940
But quantum mechanics says, if I had a supercomputer powerful enough to trap the trajectory of every single photon of light emitted by the fire,

34:49.360 --> 34:52.760
every particle of smoke, every microscopic flake of ash,

34:53.320 --> 34:58.020
I could reverse engineer the physical process and perfectly reconstruct the text of the journal.

34:58.140 --> 34:58.600
Yes.

34:58.820 --> 35:01.660
The information is heavily scrambled, but it is never destroyed.

35:01.840 --> 35:03.920
Exactly. Conservation of information is sacred.

35:03.920 --> 35:10.180
But general relativity introduced the event horizon, a region of space completely disconnected from our universe.

35:10.640 --> 35:13.220
So if you throw that handwritten journal into a black hole...

35:13.220 --> 35:16.280
It crosses the horizon. The information is isolated.

35:16.640 --> 35:21.500
Now, that wouldn't be a paradox if the black hole existed forever, just locking the information away in a vault.

35:21.720 --> 35:22.700
But they don't live forever.

35:22.920 --> 35:23.160
No.

35:23.600 --> 35:28.080
In 1974, Stephen Hawking proved that black holes don't live forever.

35:28.340 --> 35:29.160
Hawking radiation.

35:29.160 --> 35:34.740
Yes. Hawking applied quantum field theory to the curved space-time near the event horizon

35:34.740 --> 35:38.160
and discovered that black holes must emit thermal radiation.

35:38.740 --> 35:42.280
They slowly leak energy, mass, and shrink over time.

35:42.480 --> 35:42.700
Right.

35:42.860 --> 35:48.040
But the crucial detail of Hawking's calculation is that this radiation is perfectly thermal.

35:48.420 --> 35:50.040
It is completely random.

35:50.260 --> 35:54.520
It contains absolutely zero information about the matter that fell into the black hole.

35:54.520 --> 35:59.060
So the black hole radiates away its mass until it eventually evaporates into nothing.

35:59.660 --> 36:02.120
But the radiation didn't carry the journal's information out.

36:02.180 --> 36:03.040
The black hole is gone.

36:03.220 --> 36:04.300
Where did the information go?

36:04.360 --> 36:04.860
It's gone.

36:05.160 --> 36:05.720
Destroyed.

36:05.940 --> 36:08.100
Which directly violates quantum mechanics.

36:08.340 --> 36:11.960
This paradox has consumed theoretical physics for 50 years.

36:12.440 --> 36:15.500
Do we rewrite quantum mechanics to allow information destruction,

36:15.760 --> 36:20.080
or do we rewrite general relativity to figure out how information escapes the horizon?

36:20.080 --> 36:26.720
But if Flyxion's theory is right, if the dead star forms a degenerate Taurus remnant instead

36:26.720 --> 36:30.700
of a black hole, the paradox just vanishes.

36:31.000 --> 36:32.220
It evaporates completely.

36:32.480 --> 36:34.780
The paper calls this the admissibility reading.

36:35.360 --> 36:38.840
Because the DTR has no event horizon, no trapped surface ever forms,

36:39.280 --> 36:42.480
the handwritten journal never crosses a point of no return.

36:42.660 --> 36:48.260
It just falls into the gravity well and splats violently onto the ultra-dense crust of the spinning Taurus.

36:48.260 --> 36:50.560
The journal is crushed into degenerate paste.

36:50.920 --> 36:53.000
It's scrambled beyond human recognition.

36:53.860 --> 36:56.780
But the quantum information, the fundamental state of those particles,

36:57.000 --> 36:58.540
is sitting right there on the surface.

36:59.040 --> 37:03.360
It remains causally connected to the rest of the universe for its entire existence.

37:03.780 --> 37:05.340
It's a remarkably clean bypass.

37:06.000 --> 37:09.440
The geometry simply doesn't allow the paradox to instantiate in the first place.

37:09.500 --> 37:09.980
Exactly.

37:10.280 --> 37:13.080
However, the paper doesn't ignore Hawking's math entirely.

37:13.080 --> 37:17.420
In Appendix D, Flyxion explores the ultimate fate of the DTR.

37:18.200 --> 37:23.340
Because even without a true horizon, an object with a compactness that closely approximates a black hole

37:23.340 --> 37:27.480
will still possess a surface gravity that stimulates the quantum vacuum.

37:27.880 --> 37:28.200
Right.

37:28.460 --> 37:33.180
The DTR will still emit a form of Hawking radiation and slowly evaporate.

37:33.220 --> 37:34.000
Still shrinks.

37:34.360 --> 37:37.020
But the information is allowed to radiate back out with it.

37:37.020 --> 37:42.620
The paper actually provides the equations for exactly how long it takes for a DTR to evaporate.

37:43.140 --> 37:46.500
Let's look at the proportional math here because the numbers are mind-bending.

37:47.040 --> 37:49.840
The evaporation time, denoted as T-sub-Ev,

37:50.060 --> 37:54.580
is derived using the Stefan-Boltzmann law and the surface area of the object.

37:54.960 --> 37:57.740
The paper integrates the mass loss over time,

37:57.900 --> 38:03.220
and the result shows that the lifespan of the object is proportional to the cube of its initial mass.

38:03.320 --> 38:04.520
The cube of the mass.

38:04.600 --> 38:05.560
Let's make sure that's clear.

38:05.560 --> 38:10.000
If you have two of these objects, and object B is exactly twice as heavy as object A,

38:10.200 --> 38:14.640
it doesn't take twice as long to evaporate, because it's cubed two times two times two.

38:15.220 --> 38:16.740
It takes eight times as long.

38:16.940 --> 38:17.380
Exactly.

38:17.500 --> 38:19.980
If it's three times as heavy, it takes 27 times as long.

38:20.240 --> 38:23.480
The heavier they get, their lifespan extends exponentially.

38:23.840 --> 38:27.880
The paper provides a table of calculated timelines that challenge human comprehension.

38:28.580 --> 38:30.900
For a compact remnant with the mass of our sun,

38:30.900 --> 38:36.960
one solar mass complete evaporation would take roughly 2.1 times 10 to the 67th years.

38:37.340 --> 38:38.580
10 to the 67th power.

38:38.680 --> 38:38.860
Yes.

38:38.900 --> 38:39.800
Let's put that in perspective.

38:40.220 --> 38:46.360
The entire universe, from the Big Bang to right now, is only about 14 billion years old.

38:46.440 --> 38:46.640
Yeah.

38:46.760 --> 38:49.180
That's 1.4 times 10 to the 10th.

38:49.180 --> 38:56.320
So 10 to the 67th is a number so vast, it renders the age of our universe a statistical rounding error.

38:56.460 --> 39:01.220
And because of that mass-cubed relationship, supermassive objects are even more extreme.

39:01.700 --> 39:11.300
For an object of 10 billion solar masses, the size of the behemoths at the centers of major galaxies evaporation takes 2.1 times 10 to the 97th years.

39:11.300 --> 39:14.900
Which brings up a fascinating misconception that the paper explicitly corrects.

39:14.920 --> 39:15.820
Oh, about when it happens.

39:15.980 --> 39:16.120
Yeah.

39:16.600 --> 39:20.140
When people watch sci-fi or read pop science articles about black holes evaporating,

39:20.480 --> 39:24.640
they often picture it happening as an active process, like a star dies, the black hole forms,

39:24.860 --> 39:28.820
and immediately starts fizzing away into space like an aspirin dropped in a glass of water.

39:29.080 --> 39:31.900
That is a fundamental misunderstanding of thermodynamics.

39:32.740 --> 39:37.520
The paper clarifies that evaporation does not begin in earnest for trillions of years.

39:37.520 --> 39:40.980
Trillions of years from now, around 10 to the 14th years,

39:41.160 --> 39:46.060
marks the era when the last stars in the universe will burn out and star formation ceases.

39:46.400 --> 39:48.920
But even then, evaporation hasn't really begun.

39:49.080 --> 39:49.340
No.

39:49.620 --> 39:50.200
Why not?

39:50.500 --> 39:52.500
If it's emitting radiation, shouldn't it be shrinking?

39:52.920 --> 39:57.320
Because of the ambient temperature of the universe, space is not absolute zero.

39:58.220 --> 40:01.900
The cosmos is permeated by the cosmic microwave background radiation,

40:02.420 --> 40:04.320
the lingering afterglow of the Big Bang.

40:04.320 --> 40:09.540
Currently, the temperature of this background radiation is about 2.725 Kelvin.

40:09.740 --> 40:11.600
It's freezing, but it possesses heat energy.

40:11.680 --> 40:12.080
Exactly.

40:12.320 --> 40:14.980
And what is the Hawking temperature of a solar mass compact object?

40:15.180 --> 40:19.220
It is microscopically cold, roughly 6 times 10 to the negative 8 Kelvin.

40:19.500 --> 40:24.080
So the empty space of the universe is millions of times hotter than the surface of the DTR.

40:24.620 --> 40:24.960
Yes.

40:25.380 --> 40:30.000
And the laws of thermodynamics dictate that heat flows from hot to cold.

40:30.000 --> 40:33.660
The remnant is bathed in the cosmic microwave background.

40:33.660 --> 40:38.760
Right now, it is absorbing significantly more energy from the background radiation

40:38.760 --> 40:40.920
than it is emitting through Hawking radiation.

40:41.080 --> 40:42.280
It's eating the background heat.

40:42.420 --> 40:44.360
It's actually gaining mass, not losing it.

40:44.420 --> 40:44.680
Yes.

40:45.020 --> 40:45.720
It is growing.

40:46.480 --> 40:51.100
Net evaporation cannot mathematically begin until the universe expands and cools down

40:51.100 --> 40:56.440
so drastically that the background temperature drops below 6 times 10 to the negative 8 Kelvin.

40:56.440 --> 41:00.280
That won't happen until the deep, deep cosmic future.

41:00.660 --> 41:04.860
And the actual evaporation process is incredibly slow for almost its entire life, right?

41:05.500 --> 41:06.380
Excruciatingly slow.

41:06.780 --> 41:08.880
As it loses mass, its temperature rises.

41:09.180 --> 41:11.040
As it gets hotter, it radiates faster.

41:11.040 --> 41:15.620
But this runaway feedback loop doesn't become dramatic until the very end of its life.

41:15.980 --> 41:20.960
The rapid flash of evaporation where the object violently shrinks and explodes in a burst of gamma rays

41:20.960 --> 41:26.740
only occupies the absolute final fractions of a percent of that 10 to the 67th year timeline.

41:26.980 --> 41:30.120
So for almost eternity, it just sits in the dark, virtually unchanging.

41:30.300 --> 41:31.100
Basically, yes.

41:31.100 --> 41:33.760
Okay, so we have a majestic theory.

41:34.460 --> 41:39.140
We have an ultra-dense, horizonless Taurus that perfectly mimics a black hole,

41:39.620 --> 41:44.700
gracefully solves the information paradox, and is mathematically stable for octillions of years.

41:45.260 --> 41:46.260
It's a beautiful story.

41:46.300 --> 41:46.800
It is.

41:46.960 --> 41:49.300
But as we established at the beginning of this deep dive,

41:49.700 --> 41:54.060
theoretical physics is littered with beautiful stories that turned out to be complete fiction.

41:54.720 --> 41:57.040
If we can't test it, it's just philosophy.

41:57.040 --> 42:01.500
How do we spot a degenerate Taurus remnant in the real world?

42:02.200 --> 42:04.020
How do we prove Flyxion are wrong?

42:04.320 --> 42:07.720
The falsifiability program hinges entirely on gravitational waves.

42:08.220 --> 42:13.700
When two incredibly massive objects like neutron stars or these compact remnants orbit each other

42:13.700 --> 42:18.320
and eventually collide, the sheer violence of the acceleration sends ripples propagating

42:18.320 --> 42:20.360
through the fabric of space-time itself.

42:20.500 --> 42:21.240
Gravitational waves.

42:21.340 --> 42:21.820
Exactly.

42:21.820 --> 42:26.320
We can detect these microscopic stretching and squeezing effects on Earth using massive

42:26.320 --> 42:29.120
laser interferometers like LIGO and Virgo.

42:29.500 --> 42:32.640
When LIGO detects a merger, the signal has distinct phases.

42:33.120 --> 42:35.640
There's the end spiral as they orbit faster and faster.

42:36.000 --> 42:38.860
There's the merger, the massive spike in the signal when they crash.

42:39.320 --> 42:40.360
And then there's the ringdown.

42:40.700 --> 42:42.120
The ringdown is crucial.

42:42.880 --> 42:47.860
When the newly formed merged mass settles into its final state, it vibrates,

42:48.560 --> 42:51.380
radiating away the remaining gravitational wave energy.

42:51.880 --> 42:56.980
It's conceptually identical to striking a bell with a hammer and listening to the sound fade away.

42:57.260 --> 43:03.300
If standard general relativity is right and the final object is a true black hole with an event horizon,

43:03.780 --> 43:06.140
what does that ringdown look like on our instruments?

43:06.780 --> 43:09.380
An event horizon is a perfect absorber.

43:09.780 --> 43:11.740
It is the ultimate one-way membrane.

43:12.480 --> 43:17.680
Any gravitational wave energy that happens to travel inward across the horizon is trapped forever.

43:17.860 --> 43:18.920
It cannot bounce back.

43:18.920 --> 43:25.280
No. Therefore, the ringdown signal we detect on Earth decays exponentially and very quickly drops to zero.

43:25.620 --> 43:27.980
It's a clean, rapid fade to silence.

43:28.320 --> 43:30.340
But the DTR doesn't have an event horizon.

43:30.860 --> 43:35.340
It has a physical crest, a highly compressed boundary layer of degenerate matter.

43:35.520 --> 43:38.420
And that physical boundary changes the dynamics entirely.

43:38.420 --> 43:42.460
Because it is made of matter, it can support incident radiation, it can act as a mirror.

43:42.900 --> 43:45.960
Appendix C of the paper details the mechanics of this reflection.

43:46.160 --> 43:46.700
How does it work?

43:46.880 --> 43:50.100
After the initial merger and the main burst of gravitational waves,

43:50.500 --> 43:54.420
a portion of the wave energy travels inward toward the center of the remnant.

43:54.940 --> 43:59.160
It hits the surface of the DTR, reflects off it, and travels back outward.

43:59.520 --> 44:01.480
But it doesn't all escape, right?

44:01.520 --> 44:03.460
Because there's another boundary further out.

44:03.460 --> 44:06.980
Correct. Just outside the object lies the photon sphere.

44:07.500 --> 44:10.360
This is a region of intensely curved spacetime,

44:10.660 --> 44:16.240
where gravity is so strong that light, or gravitational waves, can be forced into a circular orbit.

44:16.380 --> 44:16.600
Right.

44:16.600 --> 44:20.160
The photon sphere acts as a semi-transparent barrier.

44:20.880 --> 44:24.600
When the reflected gravitational wave hits the photon sphere from the inside,

44:25.140 --> 44:28.640
some of the energy leaks out and travels across the universe to our detectors.

44:29.180 --> 44:34.320
But a significant portion of the wave reflects back inward, down to the surface of the DTR again.

44:34.440 --> 44:35.280
It's an echo chamber.

44:35.920 --> 44:38.060
The gravitational waves are trapped in a canyon,

44:38.200 --> 44:41.520
bouncing back and forth between the physical crust of the donut and the photon sphere.

44:41.520 --> 44:47.260
And with every single bounce against the photon sphere, a little bit of the wave leaks out.

44:47.580 --> 44:47.960
Precisely.

44:48.820 --> 44:52.440
On Earth, we would observe the main ring down fading away,

44:52.900 --> 44:57.340
and then, a distinct amount of time later, we would see a smaller echo in the data,

44:58.080 --> 45:02.880
followed by another delay, and an even smaller echo, repeating at regular intervals.

45:03.140 --> 45:07.960
The paper provides the math to calculate exactly how much time passes between those echoes.

45:07.960 --> 45:11.460
And the proportional relationship here is fascinating.

45:11.640 --> 45:14.480
The paper derives the round-trip echo delay time, delta t,

45:14.840 --> 45:19.580
by calculating the distance between the photon sphere and the material boundary at r sub-zero.

45:20.280 --> 45:24.040
Without getting bogged down in the calculus of the Schwarzschild-Tortoise coordinates,

45:24.580 --> 45:28.080
the final time delay is proportional to the natural logarithm of epsilon.

45:28.560 --> 45:29.760
The logarithm of epsilon.

45:29.900 --> 45:30.820
This is the crucial part.

45:31.260 --> 45:35.100
Epsilon, as we remember, is how incredibly close the outer surface of the donut is

45:35.100 --> 45:37.720
to the edge of the theoretical event horizon.

45:37.720 --> 45:42.320
The smaller epsilon gets, the closer the surface is to the cliff edge.

45:43.240 --> 45:48.300
Because it's a logarithmic relationship, as the surface gets microscopically closer to the horizon,

45:48.680 --> 45:51.460
the gravitational well gets incredibly deep.

45:52.040 --> 45:55.700
The waves have to struggle against massive space-time curvature to climb out,

45:55.940 --> 45:58.860
which makes the time delay between the echoes significantly longer.

45:59.120 --> 45:59.620
Exactly.

46:00.020 --> 46:04.360
The time delay is directly tied to the structural compactness of the object.

46:04.360 --> 46:08.980
Now, the concept of gravitational wave echoes is not unique to Flyxion's DTR.

46:09.460 --> 46:15.280
It is a general prediction for almost any exotic compact object, or eco, that lacks a horizon.

46:15.540 --> 46:20.400
But this is where Flyxion issues a blistering critique of the wider theoretical physics community.

46:20.880 --> 46:25.340
They point out the trap of unfalsifiability that plagues eco-research.

46:25.460 --> 46:26.720
It's a huge problem in the field.

46:26.720 --> 46:28.900
I love this part of the paper. Explain the trap.

46:29.000 --> 46:33.800
Okay, imagine a theorist publishes a paper proposing a new type of horizonless object,

46:34.160 --> 46:37.200
and they boldly predict it will produce gravitational wave echoes.

46:37.700 --> 46:40.160
The experimental physicists at LIGO take that prediction,

46:40.720 --> 46:44.560
analyze the data from dozens of binary mergers, and find absolutely nothing.

46:44.700 --> 46:45.160
No echoes.

46:45.620 --> 46:46.820
They publish a paper saying,

46:46.820 --> 46:50.700
We looked. The echoes aren't there. Your theory is ruled out.

46:51.300 --> 46:54.660
The system works. Hypothesis, test, rejection.

46:54.980 --> 46:56.460
But then the theorist responds,

46:56.700 --> 47:00.720
Ah, you didn't see the echoes because the surface of my object must not be very reflective.

47:01.120 --> 47:02.180
Let's just adjust my math.

47:02.720 --> 47:05.540
I'll lower the surface reflectivity parameter, R sub S,

47:05.720 --> 47:07.540
so the echoes are extremely quiet.

47:07.980 --> 47:08.860
They are still there.

47:08.980 --> 47:11.380
They are just buried in the background noise of your detectors.

47:11.860 --> 47:12.880
My theory survives.

47:13.380 --> 47:14.740
And they could do that forever.

47:14.980 --> 47:15.220
Yeah.

47:15.340 --> 47:16.160
It's infuriating.

47:16.160 --> 47:21.440
Every time LIGO gets a multi-million dollar sensitivity upgrade and still finds nothing,

47:21.860 --> 47:27.260
the theorist just turns the reflectivity dial down another notch to hide their theory in the static.

47:27.360 --> 47:27.700
Exactly.

47:28.120 --> 47:31.220
If the reflectivity parameter is left entirely unconstrained,

47:31.500 --> 47:34.240
the theorist can infinitely move the goalposts.

47:34.260 --> 47:37.440
As R subs approaches zero, the echoes vanish mathematically,

47:37.780 --> 47:41.660
and the predicted signal becomes entirely indistinguishable from a standard black hole.

47:42.000 --> 47:45.700
The theory becomes functionally immortal because it's practically unfalsifiable.

47:45.700 --> 47:48.020
It is the Texas sharpshooter fallacy.

47:48.360 --> 47:48.480
Yes.

47:48.760 --> 47:52.380
It's the guy who pulls out his revolver, shoots blindly at the side of a barn,

47:52.580 --> 47:57.920
and then walks up with a bucket of paint and draws the bullseye around wherever the bullet holes happen to be clustered.

47:58.440 --> 48:00.000
Look, a perfect shot.

48:00.060 --> 48:01.360
That's exactly what's happening.

48:01.700 --> 48:04.320
The theorists are waiting for the LIGO data to come out,

48:04.320 --> 48:09.480
and then retroactively adjusting their mathematical parameters to perfectly avoid being proven wrong.

48:09.960 --> 48:11.040
It's terrible science.

48:11.540 --> 48:14.160
Flyxion absolutely refuses to play that game.

48:14.800 --> 48:18.340
In Section 7, the paper outlines what they call the bounded criterion.

48:19.040 --> 48:21.460
They argue that to be scientifically valid,

48:21.980 --> 48:29.500
any theorist proposing a horizonless object must commit to a bounded restrictive region of parameters before any data is analyzed.

48:29.500 --> 48:33.520
They are forcing the theorists to paint the bullseye on the barn before they fire the gun.

48:33.720 --> 48:34.060
Yes.

